course: dictionary
lesson: 8902
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and shuffle is one of them. A dictionary can give several meanings, so you need the context to choose the right one. Look at the words around shuffle to find out which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «Shuffle the cards well before you deal.» Here shuffle means to mix the cards up into a random order. The clues are cards and deal, which are both about playing a card game.
explain: «The old man began to shuffle slowly down the path, dragging his feet.» Here shuffle means to walk slowly, dragging your feet along the ground. The clue is dragging his feet, so you know he is not hurrying.
explain: Use the swap test. Put your meaning into the sentence in place of the word and check that it still makes sense. If mixing the cards does not fit the sentence about the old man, try walking slowly instead.
fact: Shuffle can be a verb (to shuffle) or a noun (a shuffle).
fact: To shuffle means to mix things up, to walk with dragging feet, or to move your feet about when you are nervous or bored.
fact: In music apps, «shuffle» means the songs play in random order, as in «on shuffle».
fact: To shuffle cards means to mix them up so that no one knows what order they are in.
fact: Hint: the words around shuffle, such as cards, feet or songs, usually tell you which meaning to use.
exercise: «Shuffle the cards well before you deal.» What does shuffle mean here? | *To mix them up into a random order | To throw the cards away | To count them one by one | To hide them under the table
exercise: «The old man began to shuffle slowly down the path, dragging his feet.» What does shuffle mean? | *To walk slowly, dragging your feet | To sing loudly | To jump over the puddles | To run very fast
exercise: Mia's playlist is «on shuffle». What does that mean? | *The songs play in a random order | Only the sad songs will play | The songs stop after one song | The songs play backwards
test: Ben kept shuffling his feet under the desk while he waited. Why was he shuffling? | Because he was playing cards | *Because he was nervous or bored | Because he was very tall | Because the floor was cold
test: «Shuffle the papers before you hand them in.» What does shuffle mean here? | To count the pages | To read them aloud | *To move them about or change their order | To tear them up
exam: Which sentence uses shuffle to mean mix things into a random order? | He shuffled his feet at the front of the class | *Please shuffle the deck before the game starts | The dog shuffled across the carpet | She gave a nervous shuffle on her chair
